    "verdict": "not_a_bug",
    "summary": "T-102 asks whether the `count by` tie-break is alphabetical by design. It is. `CountBy.result` sorts on `(-count, key)` \u2014 count descending, then key ascending (src/logsift/agg.py:120), exactly as specified in SPEC.md:513 and already documented for users in README.md:116, and covered by tests for both the general ordering and the exact-tie case (tests/test_agg.py:134, tests/test_agg.py:142). With 200 and 404 at equal counts, \"200\" < \"404\" so 200 prints first \u2014 correct behavior. The reporter's requested doc change is already in place. The ticket title claims 404 has more rows, but the body clarifies the counts are equal; in the title's scenario the -count primary key would already place 404 first, so there is no defect either way. Changing the tie-break to descending key would break the documented diff-stability guarantee and would be a feature request, not a fix.",
    "evidence": [
      "src/logsift/agg.py:120",
      "src/logsift/agg.py:118",
      "SPEC.md:513",
      "README.md:116",
